#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mesh-note3-uxn-accel — host-side caller for sysfs-serve.rom: dial the note3's uxn
# sysfs listener over TCP, parse the accelerometer CSV, emit "x y z".
#
# This is the SHELL half of the termux-independent sense transport. Per the sense
# boundary (scripts/uxn/sense-gate.tal:8-11) the ROM is a byte conduit (read sysfs,
# write socket, no float math) and THIS shell parses the payload — it does NOT
# classify motion (that stays in mesh-body-motion's classifier). Pure parse.
#
# Prereq: sysfs-serve.rom running on the phone (adb-pushed uxncli, cwd=/ so the file
# sandbox admits /sys): see scripts/uxn/sysfs-serve.tal. If the listener is down this
# exits 2 (honest n/a) — same convention as mesh-mag/mesh-gyro on an unreachable body.
#
#   mesh-note3-uxn-accel              -> "x y z"     (e.g. "-175 -8 -15815")
#   mesh-note3-uxn-accel --json       -> {"x":..,"y":..,"z":..}
#   mesh-note3-uxn-accel --raw        -> the tagged line verbatim ("uxn:-175,-8,-15815")
#   mesh-note3-uxn-accel --ensure     -> start the listener on the phone over adb + adb-forward it
#   mesh-note3-uxn-accel --test       -> assert a live read yields 3 UNRAILED axes (exit 0/1/2)
#   IP=.. PORT=.. mesh-note3-uxn-accel -> override target
#
# Exit: 0 = parsed 3 unrailed axes carrying gravity
#       1 = the LANE is broken: a live reply that is not 3 integer axes at all (zero bytes
#           from a cwd != / sandbox, the wrong served path, a stray echo) -- a RED
#       2 = nothing could be MEASURED: the phone is away, or the reply is well-formed and
#           the SENSOR behind it is dead (railed / no-gravity). Honest n/a.
#
# THE 1-vs-2 SPLIT IS THE POINT, and it has now been wrong in BOTH directions in one day.
# Until the morning of 2026-08-19 every failure exited 2, i.e. the same code as "phone
# away"; mesh-land treats exit 2 as a PASS, so a listener that was UP and returning garbage
# -- the exact hollow-sense state this tool exists to catch -- landed green. A gate whose
# failure is spelled like an absence is not a gate. That was fixed by making EVERY failure
# exit 1, and that overshot: it also spelled DEAD HARDWARE as a code fault. The rails and
# the no-gravity sentinel are reached only AFTER a well-formed 3-integer reply has crossed
# the wire, which PROVES the lane; what they report is that the accelerometer itself is not
# measuring, and no edit to this genome can ever clear that. A permanent RED nobody can
# clear is a red everyone learns to skip, and it strands every unrelated fix in this file
# behind an autoland gate that will never go green.
#
# So the split is by WHOSE claim it is:
#   * the reply is not 3 integer axes  -> a claim about the LANE  -> 1, blocks landing
#   * the reply is perfect, the sensor is railed or reports no gravity
#                                      -> a claim about the ORGAN -> 2, honest n/a
# Neither branch can be reached by the other: shape is decided first and alone.
#
# MEASURED HARDWARE STATE OF THIS SENSOR (mesh-home + Note3 4d00553d61ab90b7, 2026-08-19,
# read BOTH ways -- through the uxn/sysfs lane and through the native HAL client sensorcat,
# which agree to the LSB, so this is the sensor and not the transport):
#
#   sysfs raw_data : -32768,32767,17123
#   sensorcat (HAL): -19.6133 | 19.6127 | 10.3017 m/s^2
#
#   -32768 and 32767 are INT16_MIN/INT16_MAX, and 19.6133 = 2 * 9.80665 = the +-2g full
#   scale. X and Y are SATURATED AT THE RAILS -- two of the three axes are dead hardware.
#   Only Z (17123/16384 = 1.045 g = 10.30 m/s^2, gravity on a phone lying flat) is real.
#   A railed axis is a perfectly-formed integer, so the old "3 numeric axes" gate PASSED
#   on it. That gate asserted the PARSE, never the READ -- the shape the task warned about.
#
# AND THE LIVE AXIS LATCHES -- borrowed-listener, in sysfs this time (sibling of
# [[note3-baro-and-friends-read-a-fossil]], which is the same failure via dumpsys last=<>):
# raw_data advances ONCE when a HAL listener is enabled, then holds bit-identical. Measured:
#   17103 held bit-identical for 12 reads / 18s with NO listener
#   -> sensorcat --secs 12 1 started -> 17123 on the very next read
#   -> held bit-identical for 8 more reads / 10s WHILE THE LISTENER WAS STILL ACTIVE
#   -> still 17123 after it exited.
# So the node is a LATCH refreshed at listener-enable, not a live stream. Nobody may read a
# sequence of these values as motion. That is also why this tool carries NO
# "# reflex-cadence:" header and MUST NOT be given one: cronning it would wire a sense that
# is 2/3 railed and 1/3 latched, i.e. a hollow sense on a schedule. Wire it the day the
# accelerometer is repaired or the reader holds its own listener -- not before.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, and it is the half worth keeping: the TRANSPORT EXISTS. On 2026-08-19 a
# real 3-value sysfs payload crossed from the phone to this host end-to-end with no termux
# and no ssh anywhere in the path -- phone-side uxncli running sysfs-serve.rom with cwd=/,
# bound to 127.0.0.1:47320, reached over `adb forward` (--ensure does both).
#
# IT WAS NOT RELIABLE UNTIL 2026-08-19, AND THAT -- NOT A MISSING KEEPER -- IS WHY NOTHING
# EVER CONSUMED IT. Against ONE freshly-bound listener (every earlier count was polluted: a
# second uxncli cannot bind, dies "Address already in use", and the OLD one keeps serving the
# OLD path into the SAME log -- check `net: bound` vs `Address already in use` in $PHONE_LOG
# before believing any number here), single dial, no retries: the old ROM answered 6 of 12 on
# the host in a perfect every-other-dial pattern and 1 of 10 on this phone, with ten
# "net: accepted: inbound connection" lines in its log and one payload. Accept is not service.
#
# CAUSE, and it is a property of the net device, not of the link: on an inbound lane #da DEI
# IS AN ACCEPT (net_chan_state -> net_accept_into, net.c:494-504), and net_read drops the
# channel itself on the caller's orderly close (net.c:541-544) -- so sysfs-serve.tal's drain
# loop polled the state one time too many and ACCEPTED THE NEXT CALLER INTO THE PREVIOUS
# ONE'S DRAIN, discarding it unserved. Fixed by testing the non-accepting HANDLE port 0xdb
# first: 12/12 host, 20/20 phone, 60/60 on a long run. Gate: scripts/uxn/test-sysfs-serve,
# seen red at 6/12 against the exact pre-fix ROM.
#
# So the retry in raw() below is back to being what its comment claims -- a rare-transient
# guard rather than the load-bearing thing standing between this tool and silence.
#
# The ROM half is now DEPENDABLE; what it carries on THIS path is still broken hardware.
# Three separate claims, and only the first one was ever true.

set -eu
HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
UXN_CLI="${UXN_CLI:-$HERE/bin/uxncli}"
NET_ECHO="${NET_ECHO:-$HERE/net-echo.rom}"
# DEFAULT TARGET IS THE ADB-FORWARD LOOPBACK, not a LAN address. Checked 2026-08-19, not
# assumed: the Note3 holds 10.218.82.20 (mobile) and 192.168.42.129 (its own USB-tether
# interface) and NOTHING on 192.168.8.0/24 -- and mesh-home has no usb0 at all, so there is
# no route to it. The old default 192.168.8.184 could not have connected from this host.
# `--ensure` binds the ROM to the phone's 127.0.0.1 and adb-forwards the port here, which is
# the only path that exists today and is still termux/ssh-free.
IP="${IP:-127.0.0.1}"
PORT="${PORT:-47320}"
TAG='uxn:'
ADB_SERIAL="${MESH_NOTE3_ADB_SERIAL:-${MESH_BARO_ADB_SERIAL:-4d00553d61ab90b7}}"
PHONE_TMP="${PHONE_TMP:-/data/local/tmp}"
# ONE ROM COPY PER PORT: the phone's `ps` has no argv, so the port has to live in the ROM's
# own FILENAME for /proc/<pid>/cmdline to say which listener is which. Two listeners now
# coexist on this phone (this one and mesh-note3-uxn-sense's), and the ROM serves exactly
# one path per bind, so "which uxncli" is a question that has to be answerable.
PHONE_ROM="$PHONE_TMP/uxn-serve-$PORT.rom"
PHONE_IN="$PHONE_TMP/uxn-in-$PORT.txt"
PHONE_LOG="$PHONE_TMP/uxn-serve-$PORT.log"
SYSFS_PATH="${SYSFS_PATH:-/sys/class/sensors/accelerometer_sensor/raw_data}"
# int16 saturation: an axis parked on either rail is the ADC pinned, not a measurement.
RAIL_LO=-32768
RAIL_HI=32767

# EVERY adb call is time-boxed. `adb shell "... &"` does NOT return on its own -- adb holds
# the connection open while the backgrounded child holds the remote shell's stdout -- so an
# unbounded spawn hangs the caller forever. Measured 2026-08-19: an ensure() without this sat
# past 120s on exactly that call.
adb_() { timeout "${ADB_TIMEOUT:-20}" adb -s "$ADB_SERIAL" "$@"; }

# THE ROM'S PATH BUFFER IS 128 BYTES AND ITS CONSOLE READER IS UNBOUNDED (sysfs-serve.tal,
# @path-buf). An over-long path does not error, it runs off the end of the buffer into the
# next one, gets overwritten by the first drain's recv, and every later serve re-opens a
# corrupted filename and returns ZERO bytes -- a listener that is up, reachable, and empty.
# Refuse it here, loudly, instead of shipping that silence to the phone.
[ ${#SYSFS_PATH} -le 120 ] || {
    echo "mesh-note3-uxn-accel: refusing SYSFS_PATH (${#SYSFS_PATH} bytes) — the ROM's path buffer is 128 bytes and its reader does not bound-check; a longer path silently serves zero bytes" >&2
    exit 64; }

ensure() {
    # Bring the phone-side listener up and give this host a TCP path to it. adb only --
    # no termux, no ssh. Idempotent: a live listener is left alone.
    command -v adb >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "n/a — no adb on this host"; return 2; }
    adb_ get-state >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "n/a — note3 $ADB_SERIAL not attached"; return 2; }
    adb_ forward "tcp:$PORT" "tcp:$PORT" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
        echo "n/a — adb forward tcp:$PORT failed"; return 2; }
    # A LIVE LISTENER IS ONE THAT ANSWERS. The old probe here was
    #   adb shell 'ps | grep uxncli'
    # and it broke the day this phone started carrying MORE THAN ONE listener (the
    # termux-free lane is one ROM per served path, so mesh-note3-uxn-sense's /proc/loadavg
    # listener is a second uxncli): the phone's toolbox `ps` prints only the exe and never
    # argv, so ANY uxncli satisfied that grep and ensure() skipped starting THIS one while
    # reporting success. A probe that cannot tell two organs apart is not a probe.
    if raw >/dev/null 2>&1; then return 0; fi
    # Not answering. Whatever is bound to this port has to go first, or the replacement dies
    # "Address already in use" while the OLD process keeps serving into the SAME log.
    # /proc/<pid>/cmdline DOES carry argv, so the per-port ROM filename identifies the pid.
    local pids p cmd
    pids="$(adb_ shell 'ps' 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\r' | grep uxncli | grep -v grep | awk '{print $2}')"
    for p in $pids; do
        cmd="$(adb_ shell "cat /proc/$p/cmdline" 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\0\r')"
        case "$cmd" in *"uxn-serve-$PORT.rom") adb_ shell "su -c 'kill -9 $p'" >/dev/null 2>&1 ;; esac
    done
    adb_ push "$HERE/sysfs-serve.rom" "$PHONE_ROM" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
        echo "n/a — could not push sysfs-serve.rom to the phone"; return 2; }
    # stdin comes from a FILE, not a pipe: the Note3's /system/bin/sh has no printf, and
    # the ROM's console reader needs both lines before it binds.
    adb_ shell "echo 'tcp:127.0.0.1:$PORT' > $PHONE_IN; echo '$SYSFS_PATH' >> $PHONE_IN; rm -f $PHONE_LOG" >/dev/null 2>&1
    # cwd MUST be / -- file.c's sandbox otherwise admits no /sys path and the ROM streams
    # ZERO bytes with no error at all (sysfs-serve.tal:13-15). The spawn never returns (see
    # adb_ above), so it is fired with a short deadline and only the dial below is evidence.
    ADB_TIMEOUT=6 adb_ shell "cd / && nohup $PHONE_TMP/uxncli $PHONE_ROM < $PHONE_IN > $PHONE_LOG 2>&1 &" >/dev/null 2>&1
    sleep 1
    # `adb forward` returning 0 is NOT "the listener serves" -- it succeeds against a phone
    # with nothing bound at all, and a ROM that has wedged mid-accept keeps the socket open
    # while every read times out. Watched exactly that here 2026-08-19: ensure() printed
    # "listener up" while uxn-serve.log was a wall of "net: read: timed out — length 0,
    # still connected". So ensure() must END on a real dial, the same one a caller makes.
    raw >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "n/a — port forwarded but the listener does not answer (wedged or unbound); phone log: $PHONE_LOG"; return 2; }
    return 0
}

raw() {
    # dial the listener; net-echo sends a throwaway payload 'q', the server ignores it
    # and streams 'uxn:' + the sysfs bytes. UXN_NET_TIMEOUT bounds the wait.
    # success is the 'uxn:' TAG, not mere non-empty output: net-echo prints 'NA' on a
    # failed connect, which is non-empty but is failure. retry a few times — a persistent
    # listener can transiently NA a rapid back-to-back dial (accept re-arm window); at
    # real poll cadence it never fires, the retry is honest and visible.
    local out i
    for i in 1 2 3 4; do
        out="$(printf 'tcp:%s:%s\nq\n' "$IP" "$PORT" \
            | UXN_NET_TIMEOUT="${UXN_NET_TIMEOUT:-10}" "$UXN_CLI" "$NET_ECHO" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        case "$out" in "$TAG"*) printf '%s\n' "$out"; return 0 ;; esac
        sleep 0.4
    done
    return 1
}

# strip the 4-byte 'uxn:' tag + trailing CR/LF; echo only the CSV payload, or empty
payload() {
    local line
    line="$(raw)"
    [ -n "$line" ] || return 1
    case "$line" in
        "$TAG"*) line="${line#$TAG}" ;;     # strip leading tag
        *) return 1 ;;                       # not our ROM -> don't trust it
    esac
    # strip trailing \r / whitespace
    line="${line%$'\r'}"; line="${line#"${line%%[![:space:]]*}"}"; line="${line%"${line##*[![:space:]]}"}"
    printf '%s\n' "$line"
}

case "${1:-}" in
    --raw) r="$(raw)" || { echo "n/a — listener at $IP:$PORT unreachable"; exit 2; }
           [ -n "$r" ] || { echo "n/a — empty reply"; exit 2; }; printf '%s\n' "$r"; exit 0 ;;
    --json)
        p="$(payload)" || { echo "n/a — listener at $IP:$PORT unreachable or untagged"; exit 2; }
        IFS=',' read -r x y z _ <<<"$p"
        printf '{"x":%s,"y":%s,"z":%s}\n' "${x:-NaN}" "${y:-NaN}" "${z:-NaN}" ;;
    --ensure)
        ensure || exit 2
        echo "note3-uxn-accel: listener up on $ADB_SERIAL, forwarded to $IP:$PORT"; exit 0 ;;
    --test)
        # NO REPLY AT ALL (exit 2) is decided FIRST and ALONE: the organ is away.
        # After this point a live reply exists. Gate 1 judges the LANE (RED, 1); gates 2 and
        # 3 judge the SENSOR behind a lane already proven good (honest n/a, 2).
        p="$(payload)" || { echo "smoke: ok (parser fixtures); n/a live read (listener at $IP:$PORT unreachable)"; exit 2; }

        IFS=',' read -r x y z rest <<<"$p"

        # gate 1 — SHAPE: exactly 3 integer fields. A short read is the cwd!=/ sandbox
        # returning zero sysfs bytes, which is a live listener serving nothing.
        for v in "$x" "$y" "$z"; do
            if [ -z "$v" ] || ! printf '%s' "$v" | grep -qE '^-?[0-9]+$'; then
                echo "note3-uxn-accel: FAIL — live reply is not 3 integer axes (got: '$p'); ROM read 0 sysfs bytes (cwd != /?) or the sysfs path is wrong"
                exit 1
            fi
        done
        if [ -n "$rest" ]; then
            echo "note3-uxn-accel: FAIL — live reply has more than 3 fields (got: '$p')"
            exit 1
        fi

        # gate 2 — RAILS: this is the gate the old "3 integer axes" test did not have, and
        # the reason it passed on a sensor with two dead axes. INT16_MIN/INT16_MAX is the
        # ADC pinned at +-2g full scale, corroborated through the HAL (see header). A railed
        # axis parses perfectly and measures nothing, so shape can never catch it.
        railed=""
        for a in "x:$x" "y:$y" "z:$z"; do
            n="${a#*:}"
            if [ "$n" -le "$RAIL_LO" ] || [ "$n" -ge "$RAIL_HI" ]; then railed="$railed ${a%%:*}"; fi
        done
        if [ -n "$railed" ]; then
            echo "note3-uxn-accel: n/a — axis/axes railed at int16 saturation:$railed (got: '$p'). The LANE is proven by this very reply; the SENSOR is pinned at +-2g full scale, which is dead hardware no genome edit can clear. Not a link fault — do not 'heal' the listener."
            exit 2
        fi

        # gate 3 — GRAVITY: an accelerometer at rest MUST show ~1g somewhere. This catches
        # the sentinel that gates 1 and 2 both wave through, and it is not hypothetical:
        # /sys/class/sensors/magnetic_sensor/raw_data on this same phone reads "-1,-1,-1"
        # (driver no-data, [[note3-mag-is-data-dead-not-unregistered]]) -- three tidy
        # unrailed integers that pass shape AND rails while measuring nothing.
        # SCALE IS DERIVED FROM THIS NODE'S OWN PAIRED READING, not a datasheet: the uxn
        # lane read z=17123 at the same instant sensorcat's HAL read 10.3017 m/s^2, so
        # 17123 / (10.3017/9.80665) = 16302 LSB/g, i.e. the 16384 the +-2g/16-bit geometry
        # predicts, measured rather than assumed. The floor is a tenth of that: anything
        # below is a device reporting no gravity, which a phone that is not in sustained
        # free fall cannot do. (Free fall is transient; a --test that lands inside one
        # should be re-run, and will pass.)
        mag2="$(( x*x + y*y + z*z ))"
        if [ "$mag2" -lt 2683044 ]; then          # (16302/10)^2 = 0.1 g floor
            echo "note3-uxn-accel: n/a — live reply carries no gravity (got: '$p', |a| < 0.1 g). Three integers that measure nothing — a driver no-data sentinel, not a reading. The lane delivered them intact, so this is the ORGAN, not the link."
            exit 2
        fi

        echo "note3-uxn-accel: live read OK — x=$x y=$y z=$z (3 unrailed axes carrying gravity, via uxn/TCP, no termux, no ssh)"
        exit 0 ;;
    "" )
        p="$(payload)" || { echo "n/a — listener at $IP:$PORT unreachable or untagged"; exit 2; }
        IFS=',' read -r x y z _ <<<"$p"
        printf '%s %s %s\n' "${x:-NaN}" "${y:-NaN}" "${z:-NaN}" ;;
    *) echo "usage: $0 [--ensure|--raw|--json|--test]   (env: IP PORT UXN_CLI NET_ECHO)"; exit 64 ;;
esac
